# Service layer

`ClinicServiceImpl` is a flat facade over all six domain repositories. It holds almost no
business logic; its real job is to be the **transaction boundary**.

## Rules

1. Annotate every method: `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` for reads, `@Transactional` for
   writes. This is the only place transactions are declared.
2. **Return `null` for "not found", never throw.** Controllers rely on this to emit 404. Follow
   the existing shape exactly, because the three DAO profiles disagree about absence:

   ```java
   @Override
   @Transactional(readOnly = true)
   public Owner findOwnerById(int id) throws DataAccessException {
       Owner owner = null;
       try {
           owner = ownerRepository.findById(id);
       } catch (ObjectRetrievalFailureException | EmptyResultDataAccessException e) {
           return null;   // normalise Spring Data vs JPA/JDBC behaviour
       }
       return owner;
   }
   ```

3. **Two service calls are two persistence contexts.** Entities returned by separate calls are
   distinct instances. Never compare them with `equals()` — `BaseEntity` does not override it,
   so the comparison is reference identity. This is the cause of defect `B1`:

   ```java
   // ❌ BAD — always false across two service calls
   if (!pet.getOwner().equals(owner)) { ... }

   // ✅ GOOD — compare ids
   if (!pet.getOwner().getId().equals(owner.getId())) { ... }
   ```

4. Keep new logic thin here. Validation belongs in the DTO/Bean Validation layer; persistence
   detail belongs in the repository.
5. `UserServiceImpl.saveUser` currently stores passwords **unencoded**. Do not copy that
   pattern; see `AUTH-03` before touching credentials.
